                <h2>Why a Three-Page Appeal Still Gets Rejected</h2>
<p>Google Ads review teams are not short on appeals; they are short on <strong>verifiable evidence</strong>. Most rejected submissions follow the same pattern: a self-explanation letter, one screenshot, and a few lines claiming the product is legitimate. Such materials neither reference specific policy clauses nor answer the three questions reviewers actually care about: <strong>Who are you, what do you sell, and why are you compliant?</strong></p>
<p>The core principle for preparing an appeal is to turn it from an emotional statement into a chain of evidence.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Identify the Appeal Type and Use the Right Channel</h2>
<p>Misrouted appeals never reach the correct review queue, no matter how strong the documents are.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Account disabled with "no active ad series"</strong>: focus on account ownership and business legitimacy.</li>
<li><strong>Policy disapproval (individual ads)</strong>: address the specific ad, with landing page and creative documentation.</li>
<li><strong>Full account suspension</strong>: demonstrate that no circumvention occurred.</li>
<li><strong>Risk verification / identity verification</strong>: submit requested IDs, checking expiry date and image clarity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Checklist:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Confirm the exact flagged reason under "Admin → Account status" or in the notification email</li>
<li>[ ] Determine whether the issue is account-level or ad-level; the material lists differ</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm whether to use the account appeal form or the ad review page</li>
<li>[ ] Record the first violation date, rejected ad IDs, and the original notification text</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 2: Assemble the Evidence Package by Type</h2>
<h3>A. Suspended Account</h3>
<ul>
<li>Business license / registration certificate (English or with translation)</li>
<li>Links to your website product page, pricing page, and terms of service</li>
<li>Proof of payment method (credit card statement or bank statement showing company name and address)</li>
<li>Proof of app or website ownership: GA4 / Google Analytics access, Play Console developer account, App Store developer console screenshots</li>
<li>If an agency manages the account: agency mandate letter, client authorization, and agency contract</li>
</ul>
<h3>B. Disapproved Ads</h3>
<ul>
<li>A specific explanation of the issue, mapped to the policy name and clause</li>
<li>The landing page URL plus screenshots (including the final URL after redirects)</li>
<li>Original ad copy, images, and video files</li>
<li>Qualification documents: licenses for pharma, permits for financial services, licenses and geographic restrictions for gambling</li>
<li>Product detail page, pricing, and inventory screenshots</li>
</ul>
<h3>C. Payment / Chargeback Risk</h3>
<ul>
<li>Refund policy, shipping and delivery timelines</li>
<li>Summary of the last three months of orders and refunds (anonymized but verifiable)</li>
<li>Description of your customer service and dispute-handling process</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Write the Appeal Body Using a Four-Part Structure</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>One-sentence conclusion</strong>: You understand the reason for the action and have taken corrective steps.</li>
<li><strong>Facts</strong>: business model, target audience, and campaign objectives, stated objectively without emotion.</li>
<li><strong>Point-by-point policy response</strong>: one paragraph per policy, citing clause numbers or original keyword.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence and commitment</strong>: list attachment numbers and state concrete actions taken (e.g., landing page updated, certain ad series paused).</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep the body between 300 and 600 words and reference every attachment number in the text. Overly long appeals are often deprioritized.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Pre-Submission Self-Check</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] All links are publicly accessible without login, worldwide, and not redirect chains</li>
<li>[ ] Documents are within validity period, clear, glare-free, and not cropped</li>
<li>[ ] Business license name, payment method, and landing page company details match exactly (spelling, capitalization, address)</li>
<li>[ ] Landing pages contain no phrases such as "click here to top up," "guaranteed returns," or "100% approval"</li>
<li>[ ] Attachments are named systematically: <code>01_BusinessLicense.pdf</code>, <code>02_Website.png</code></li>
<li>[ ] One primary issue per submission; do not combine five appeal types into one letter</li>
<li>[ ] If no response in 7–14 days, follow up by email and quote the original case number</li>
</ul>
<h2>Common Rejection Reasons at a Glance</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Symptom</th>
<th>Frequent cause</th>
<th>Corrective direction</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Disabled with no active campaigns</td>
<td>Website ownership unverifiable</td>
<td>Add GA4 / Search Console access proof</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Full suspension</td>
<td>Multiple accounts sharing one card with identical creatives</td>
<td>Explain agency-client relationships; supply contracts and client list</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Repeated disapprovals</td>
<td>Landing page inconsistent with ad claims</td>
<td>Align copy and page content; add qualification pages</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Repeated identity failure</td>
<td>Expired document or expired QR code</td>
<td>Re-upload clear files and verify name spelling</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>An Anonymous Example</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team was flagged for circumvention because several affiliated accounts shared the same credit card, and the account was suspended. The team rebuilt the package: main operating business license, credit card statement showing the company name, proof of brand authorization chain, five sub-client media buying contracts, and written confirmations from those clients. The account was restored three weeks later, though four ad series had to be resubmitted for review.</p>
<p>The key step almost nobody takes was the <strong>written client confirmation</strong> — it converts "we did not violate anything" into a verifiable fact.</p>
<h2>Managing the Timeline After Submission</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep an internal ticket table: submission date, review cycle, follow-up milestones</li>
<li>Pause similar high-risk operations during the review window to avoid a second violation</li>
<li>Within 24 hours of approval, re-check payment method, spending permissions, conversion tracking, and the domain remarketing list</li>
<li>If rejected, do not resubmit unchanged; check whether the reason matches the original one before adding evidence or changing strategy</li>
</ul>
<p>If your account is currently in a top-up or low-balance warning state, isolate single accounts during the appeal to avoid freezing the overall balance due to abnormal multi-account behavior on the same entity — see the top-up and balance management notes on the Yiyu platform for details.</p>
